## Goal
Fix DEV-25: `portero default set` failed with "https: The file couldn't be opened" even after the macOS dialog had already set both schemes. Make the command idempotent and quiet, while a real failure stays an error.

## Problems
- The earlier candidate commit only checked the handler after a call when macOS returned an error. If macOS returned nil but the scheme stayed foreign (dialog declined), it printed success with exit 0.
- No unit tests existed for `cmd/portero`, because the logic called cgo handler functions directly.
- The macOS error on a repeat call carries no useful information (Launch Services permission-denied underneath).
- The installed app predates the fix, so it was not verified live.
- CI does not run `go test`; the gate is local `make test`.

## Decision
- Extract `ensureDefault(app, current, set)`: skip schemes already ours, call set otherwise, then unconditionally re-read the actual handler. A scheme still foreign is an error that keeps the macOS error text and stops the loop. A set error is ignored if the scheme is ours afterwards.
- Extract `printDefaultStatus` writing to an `io.Writer`, with "already set" / "set now" notes.
- Unit tests with fakes cover six scenarios plus the status format.
- Use a new commit on top of the candidate rather than an amend.
- Cross-review by Codex. The user chose the full live check (via Safari, with dialog), which the user runs.

## Rejected
- Trusting the macOS error value: not informative on repeat calls.
- A polling loop for NSWorkspace cache staleness: deferred unless seen live.

## Touched
macOS Launch Services / NSWorkspace, cgo `internal/handler`, `cmd/portero`, Go toolchain, Makefile, Linear (DEV-25, DEV-26), Codex cross-review.
